  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\n- Rain Showers Late Tonight into Thursday\n\nShortwave trough moving through currently has sparked off some\nisolated light showers and sprinkles. Any light shower should\nclear the area by daybreak with skies clearing.\n\nAnother shortwave trough moves through the northern Great Lakes\ntonight into Thursday. Associated positive vorticity advection\nseems weaker, but mid level frontogenesis and low to mid level\nmoisture with the front are sufficient enough for scattered\nshowers to develop. These showers will move into the Ludington \narea around midnight and gradually shift southeast with the front \ninto Thursday morning. Showers may linger through Thursday \nafternoon along the I-94 corridor before dry conditons return \nThursday night.\n\nHigh temperatures today will be in the low 70s with lows in the\n50s. Thursday behind the front temperatures will only warm into\nthe 60s with low 70s across southern and south central Michigan.\nTemperatures Thursday night into Friday drop into the 30s.\n\n- Slight Chances (20 to 30 percent) for Rain This Weekend\n\nEnsembles and cluster analysis are trending drier for Saturday as\nhigh pressure holds a little longer over the Great Lakes region.\nThere are still some solutions that bring light rain to the area,\nbut the number of ensemble members has dropped over the past 24\nhours. Current forecast has a 20 percent chance for southwest\nMichigan.\n\nSunday the cluster analysis begins to diverge on the overall\npattern. There is an increase in members showing light rainfall\namounts over the area with positive vorticity streaming through\nand increased mid level moisture. Best chances 30 to 40 percent\nare along and south of I-96 with amounts less that a quarter of an\ninch.\n\n- Unsettled Pattern Next Week\n\nAs mentioned in the previous section model solutions as seen in\nthe cluster analysis diverge with lower confidence on the exact\nforecast for next week. A closed low is anticipated to develop\nover the Plains, but the exact strength, track, and timing vary.\nSouthwest flow and the looming low could bring multiple rounds of\nrain to the area through most of next week. The chance for below \nfreezing temperatures next week remains low as mild temperatures \nremain in place.",
